Building and testing direct DRI3 CoPresent
===========================================

Scope
-----

CoPresent has two separately built pieces. Keeping that boundary clear is
important: rebuilding only cogpu-daemon is not enough once the Mesa client
path is selected, and the two halves share a wire format.

  1. `cogpu-daemon.exe` owns the Windows side. It decodes the guest's virgl
     command stream, and when that stream carries `VIRGL_CCMD_MOCO_PRESENT` it
     resolves the named resource to its existing host GL texture and displays
     it in an overlay above the stock VcXsrv window.
  2. A patched Mesa -- `libGL`, `libEGL`, `libGLESv2`, the Gallium DRI library
     and `libgbm`, built for BOTH ABIs -- makes ordinary GLX and EGL clients
     consume Mesa's normal DRI3 provider, render with virgl on
     `/dev/dri/renderD128`, and emit that presentation command at the point a
     Linux X server would have been handed a dma-buf.

Presentation rides the command stream deliberately. It travels down the render
node the client already has open, so nothing else has to be reachable: no
socket, no extra device, no filesystem share. That is what makes sandboxed
clients work with no configuration at all -- Firefox's content processes and
Steam's pressure-vessel container cannot see `/run`, and the earlier
broker-socket revision was invisible to both, silently falling back to software
while unsandboxed clients worked.

The broker, its pinned ring, its dma-buf export/PRIME import and its root-only
pagemap requirement are gone. `moco-present-broker` and `moco-present.service`
are no longer built, installed or enabled; a guest carrying them from an older
release can have them removed.

VcXsrv is not rebuilt or patched. It remains the X control plane and owns
the top-level Windows window, input, focus and ordinary X drawing.

A note on Vulkan: virgl provides OpenGL only, and there is no Vulkan in this
stack. The modern Steam client will not build its UI without a Vulkan device,
so the root filesystem installs the distribution's `vulkan-swrast` (lavapipe)
for both ABIs. That answers the client's probe in software while games keep
rendering through accelerated GL. Accelerated Vulkan is a separate project.

Authoritative sources
---------------------

The host and broker sources are in this repository:

    src/colinux/os/winnt/user/cogpu-daemon/{copresent,present}.{c,h}
    src/colinux/user/copresent/

The Mesa input is the official 26.1.6 release tarball:

    https://archive.mesa3d.org/mesa-26.1.6.tar.xz
    SHA256 5296b88a0f1e012e2cb9ada150a2bbadf728ca81e5a4fb2ab43c83a4d2158606

The complete Mesa delta is:

    patch/mesa-26.1.6/moco-copresent.diff
    SHA256 2d0b1f0b024c3fe7457782d166d8970393d699a3bec61e1386a6563fb770743e

It contains every Mesa change, including all new source/header files.
It applies with `patch -p1 --fuzz=0`; no file from the modified development
tree under `download/mesa-26.1.6` is required.

Build the Windows side
----------------------

Use the normal project build, from the repository root:

    tools/build.sh

That invokes the existing `bin/make.py` build, signs `linux.sys`, builds the
installer, and stages `cogpu-daemon.exe`, virglrenderer, libepoxy and the
launchers together in `../dist-x64`. Building cogpu-daemon directly is useful
for compiler diagnostics, but its raw build directory is not an install.

The R2 daemon is opt-in. After stopping the existing guest with `stop.bat`
and ensuring a leftover `cogpu-daemon.exe` is gone, launch the installed VBS
with the normal binary and image directories followed by `--present-r2`:

    wscript C:\PROGRA~1\MoCoLinux\moco-boot.vbs C:\PROGRA~1\MoCoLinux C:\MoCoLinux --present-r2

The host log is `%SystemDrive%\MoCoLinux\cogpu-daemon.log`. Per-command
`--verbose` tracing must stay off during performance tests: it logs every
fenced command and measurably throttles presentation.

Build the Mesa GL stack
-----------------------

The canonical build is one command:

    MOCO_BUILD_JOBS=16 tools/build-mesa-copresent.sh

The script:

  * verifies the release tarball SHA-256 before extracting it;
  * refuses patch fuzz and records the applied patch hash;
  * creates a Python virtual environment with Meson 1.11.2, Mako 1.4.1,
    MarkupSafe 3.0.3, PyYAML 6.0.3 and packaging 26.3;
  * configures Mesa's normal DRI GLX provider and Gallium virgl/softpipe, with
    EGL, GBM and GLES2 ENABLED and GLVND, Vulkan, LLVM, VA and tests disabled;
  * builds `libGL`, `libEGL`, `libGLESv2`, the Gallium DRI library, `libgbm`
    and its DRI backend with Ninja; and
  * repeats the whole build for i686 using `tools/i686-cross.ini`, stripping
    each ABI into `../build/mesa-copresent-26.1.6/out/lib64` and `.../lib32`.

EGL and GLES are built rather than disabled because modern clients ask for
them: with no EGL of ours present, Firefox's hardware WebRender and Steam's CEF
helper resolved to the distribution's Mesa, probed real DRI3 against the
Windows X server and fell back to software.

The 32-bit half is not optional in practice. Steam's client is i386 and asks
the 32-bit loader for both GL and Vulkan; without it the client renders in
software and its Vulkan probe fails with `VK_ERROR_INCOMPATIBLE_DRIVER`. The
script skips that half rather than failing when the build host has no multilib
toolchain, and says so; set `MOCO_MESA_32BIT=0` to skip it deliberately.

The build host needs a C/C++ toolchain, Python venv support, Ninja, patch,
pkg-config and the development files Meson reports for X11/XCB, libdrm,
expat, zlib, zstd, sensors and SPIR-V Tools -- and, for the 32-bit half,
`gcc -m32` plus the i686 development libraries for the same set. The resulting
libraries are dynamically linked, so the guest must provide matching runtime
libraries. The accepted build host and guest both use glibc 2.44.

Useful overrides are:

    MOCO_MESA_TARBALL   path to the verified 26.1.6 tarball
    MOCO_MESA_WORK      persistent source/build/venv directory
    MOCO_MESA_VENV      an existing or new Python virtual environment
    MOCO_MESA_OUT64     final stripped 64-bit output directory
    MOCO_MESA_OUT32     final stripped 32-bit output directory
    MOCO_MESA_32BIT     set to 0 to skip the 32-bit half
    MOCO_MESA_PREFIX    embedded Mesa data/config prefix (default `/usr`)
    MOCO_BUILD_JOBS     Ninja worker count

The script never deletes an old work tree. If its saved patch hash differs,
point `MOCO_MESA_WORK` at a fresh directory instead of silently stacking a
new patch on an old source tree.

Test without changing the guest graphics stack
-----------------------------------------------

Copy both matched outputs into the guest, then select them for just the
diagnostic process. `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` here is only an isolated development-
library selector; CoPresent does not use `LD_PRELOAD`, VirtualGL or a faker
library.

    mkdir -p /tmp/moco-lib
    ln -sf /path/to/libGL-moco.so.1 /tmp/moco-lib/libGL.so.1
    cp /path/to/libgallium-26.1.6.so /tmp/moco-lib/
    DISPLAY=:0 LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/tmp/moco-lib glxinfo -B
    DISPLAY=:0 LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/tmp/moco-lib glxgears -geometry 1600x837

Expected identity is `direct rendering: Yes`, renderer `virgl`, and Mesa
26.1.6. Neither command is run through `moco-gl` or `vglrun`.

Opt-in system selection and rollback
------------------------------------

For normal command lines during direct-DRI3 development, copy
`tools/install-mesa-copresent.sh` into the guest and run:

    sudo ./install-mesa-copresent.sh enable /path/to/build-output-directory
    # or: enable /path/to/libGL-moco.so.1 /path/to/libgallium-26.1.6.so
    ./install-mesa-copresent.sh status
    glxinfo -B

This installs under `/usr/local/lib/moco-copresent` and adds an ldconfig
directory. It does not overwrite a package-owned Mesa or GLVND file. To
roll back immediately:

    sudo ./install-mesa-copresent.sh disable

The accepted guest test resolved normal `glxinfo` to
`/usr/local/lib/moco-copresent/libGL.so.1.2.0`, loaded the matched Gallium
library, reported direct virgl, and kept both package-owned Mesa and the old R2
prototype intact for rollback. The selector archives the old higher-versioned
`libGL.so.1.5.0` outside the ldconfig directory so it cannot silently win over
the DRI library.

The shipped root filesystem installs this stack by default, for both ABIs, from
`../dist-guest/lib64` and `../dist-guest/lib32` -- see the GPU section of
`tools/mkmanjarorootfs.sh`. The reversible selector above remains the way to
test a build without rebuilding an image.

Accepted hardware result, 2026-08-10 (command-stream transport)
--------------------------------------------------------------

Measured on the same Windows 10 / GeForce GT 730 host, with the broker service
stopped and `/run/moco-present.sock` deleted, proving the socket is no longer
part of the path:

    64-bit  tools/glbench  1280x720  543 fps
    32-bit  tools/glbench  1280x720  602 fps

The host log recorded 3,170 presented frames over the same interval, so the
frames were drawn rather than silently dropped. `glxgears` reported ~275 fps and
`glxinfo -B` direct virgl throughout.

Sandboxed clients were the point of the change and were checked as such. Steam's
own `console-linux.txt` logs `MoCo DRI3: direct virgl/CoPresent active` for
three drawables on every launch, from inside its pressure-vessel container, with
no environment variable and no container configuration. Firefox presents
directly and remains interactive; the overlay is created `WS_EX_LAYERED |
WS_EX_TRANSPARENT` so clicks reach VcXsrv beneath it. Returning `HTTRANSPARENT`
from `WM_NCHITTEST` is NOT sufficient there -- that only forwards the hit test
to windows of the same thread, and VcXsrv is another process, so an earlier
non-layered overlay swallowed every click while rendering correctly.

Outstanding: Steam's own UI still does not appear. Its CEF helper fails in
`clienthandler.cpp` with "Failed to create browser window" -- with GPU
initialisation clean, a Vulkan device enumerated and disk space free. The same
failure occurs with this stack disabled and the distribution's Mesa in use, so
it is not a CoPresent defect; it is CEF against VcXsrv 1.14.

Accepted hardware result, 2026-08-09
------------------------------------

On the Windows 10 / GeForce GT 730 test host, the normal DRI3 build reported:

    direct rendering: Yes
    OpenGL renderer: virgl (NVIDIA GeForce GT 730/PCIe/SSE2)
    OpenGL 4.2 (Core and Compatibility Profile) Mesa 26.1.6

`LIBGL_DEBUG=verbose glxinfo -B` logged `Using DRI3 with MoCo CoPresent for
screen 0`. VcXsrv 1.14 initially answered Mesa's optional raw-window
`X_GLXGetDrawableAttributes` probe with fatal GLXBadDrawable; the final patch
uses ordinary X window attributes for that inference only in MoCo mode. No
VcXsrv file changed.

Unmodified `glxgears` then completed consecutive five-second samples of 1,343
and 1,412 frames, or 268.6 and 282.2 fps. While it ran, `/proc/PID/maps`
contained:

    /usr/local/lib/moco-copresent/libGL.so.1.2.0
    /usr/local/lib/moco-copresent/libgallium-26.1.6.so

There was no libvgl or faker mapping. The earlier R2 Xlib prototype is kept in
history, but these measurements are from the normal `-Dglx=dri` provider.

For a non-trivial load, unmodified `glxspheres64` rendered 3,513,600 polygons
per frame at 1920x1080 and sustained roughly 15.5 fps. Windows performance
counters measured 81--87% utilization on cogpu-daemon.exe's 3D engine. This
proves that the ordinary GLX client is executing on the real host GPU; it does
not claim native efficiency. The present path currently waits the acquire
fence, draws the sampled texture, calls `glFinish`, completes `SwapBuffers`,
returns RELEASE and only then lets the producer continue. Removing that
one-frame-in-flight serialization is the next implementation step.

The standalone two-buffer producer also submitted and received RELEASE for
all 116,587 frames in a 126-second interactive resize/destruction run. It
rebound at 1000x700, 1200x760, 800x500 and 1500x800, and the host recorded a
matched UNBIND for both buffers at each size and on normal window closure. The
uninterrupted ten-minute soak in `doc/direct-presentation` remains outstanding.

After the runtime test, a new clean build was made solely from the official
tarball and the repository patch using the canonical script. That pair was
installed with the reversible selector; its installed hashes matched the build
outputs and unmodified `glxinfo -B` again completed through direct DRI3 virgl.
The two stripped outputs were:

    37a0d93b3b9f49d4038cbd31a51316ab71495b406a45bceaf3e291f36a5d7bec  libGL-moco.so.1
    6f845c23ebf579293008a5367c85bd100f2efc6992ce4ced96290e5ad9c0d2b2  libgallium-26.1.6.so

Those output hashes are evidence for this toolchain and dependency set, not a
promise that every compiler will produce byte-identical ELF files. The
release tarball checksum and a zero-fuzz application of the repository patch
are the reproducible source boundary.
